-- title:
--   K(j(q),j(q^N)) equals K(j(qᵈ):d∣ N) in characteristic zero
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field of characteristic zero and let $N$ be a nonzero natural number. Inside the field of formal Laurent series $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\ K$ in the variable $q$, consider the element `jqModC K`, namely $q^{-1}$ times the image under coefficientwise reduction $\mathbb{Z} \to K$ of the power series `jNum` (so the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$), and its substitution $q \mapsto q^N$, written `jqNModC K N` $=$ `qExpand K N (jqModC K)`. The theorem asserts the equality of two intermediate fields of $K \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\ K$: the field `modularFunctionFieldC K N` obtained by adjoining to $K$ the two elements `jqModC K` and `jqNModC K N`, and the field `modularFunctionFieldFullC K N` obtained by adjoining to $K$ the whole set `divisorExpansionsC K N` of those Laurent series of the form `qExpand K d (jqModC K)` for some nonzero natural number $d$ dividing $N$. Thus $K(j(q), j(q^N)) = K\bigl(j(q^d) : d \mid N,\ d \neq 0\bigr)$ as subfields of $K((q))$.
--
--   This is the characteristic-zero case of the classical generation statement for the function field of $X_0(N)$: the field generated by the $q$-expansions $j(q^d)$, $d \mid N$, is already generated by the two expansions $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$. It is used as the input for the corresponding comparison in characteristic $p$ and, downstream, for computations of degrees of the fields obtained by adjoining `jqNModC` over modular function fields at various levels.
-- source:
